title: The watch budget
tags: [[Watcher]]
kind: reference
aliases: mfr_watch_exceeded
summary: inotify costs one kernel watch per directory from a per-user supply; the daemon caps its share, records the subtrees it could not afford as mfr_watch_exceeded, and reports a kernel refusal as a state.

inotify holds one watch per directory — there is no recursive watch — so a tree of N
eligible directories costs N kernel watches out of `fs.inotify.max_user_watches`, a
''per-user'' supply shared with every other program that watches files, each watch
costing about a kilobyte of unswappable kernel memory. (This is the budget regime's
own: the coverage regime has nothing per directory to budget.)

''The share.'' `[settings] watch-budget-share` (percent, default 50) caps what one
daemon spends: `cap = max_user_watches × share ÷ 100` ([[Daemon configuration]]). A
self-imposed ceiling, not a reservation — the kernel exposes the limit, not what is
free.

!! Two different failures

* ''Our cap is reached'': the repository wants more than this daemon will spend — a lasting property of this repository on this machine, so it is ''recorded'': `mfr_watch_exceeded` on the frontier.
* ''The kernel refuses (`ENOSPC`) under our cap'': something else took the watches — transient and external, so ''nothing is written''; the placement stops there and `watch_budget.starved` says so for as long as it lasts (a state, not a message — the 500-entry diagnostics ring would evict the notice under its own repetitions). The repos panel shows it in red: nothing was recorded, and the remedy is outside metafolder.

!! mfr_watch_exceeded

A reserved field, inherited down `mfr_path` like `mf_watch` — the nearest ancestor
defining it wins, a value set directly overrides. `true` means ''leave this subtree
uncovered by the watcher'', and every regime honours it: the budget regime places no
watch there, the coverage regime records no event under it.

It means nothing else: the subtree stays tracked — reconcile walks it, queries return
it, the scans consider it, `mf_watch` still says what you ''want'' watched. Changes there
are noticed by the next reconcile instead of immediately.

* The daemon writes it only on the ''frontier'' — the subtree roots the placement did not enter, never every directory beneath — which is what keeps it affordable in exactly the case that triggers it. Only the budget regime writes it on its own.
* It is ''sticky'': never cleared by the daemon, so the uncovered set is stable across sessions and yours to reshape. Grown headroom shows in `watch_budget`; reclaiming it is explicit.
* Writing it takes effect at once (re-placement, or a new event filter).
* Setting it is always allowed. Clearing it is refused with `409` in the budget regime when there is no headroom, naming what to set first; under coverage it is always allowed.
* It does not travel through sync: what is left uncovered is a fact about one machine.

```
mf -n big watch exceeded                   # the subtrees carrying it, with the budget line
mf -n big watch exceeded set /archive      # leave /archive uncovered, freeing its watches
mf -n big watch exceeded clear /archive    # cover it again (409 without headroom)
```

`mf watch status` adds a line when the budget is in trouble; the repos panel shows
`N subtree(s) unwatched` (amber, a button listing them — `repos:watch-exceeded`). It is
not a remedy for a noisy directory — that is `mf_watch = false` or a pattern
([[Watch activity]]). Tested by lowering `watch-budget-share` until an ordinary fixture
exceeds it.
